  "evidence": "TEXT_F: NHS funding to rise in real terms with more GPs and nurses; protect homeowners via low inflation and lower mortgage rates; national fight against drugs and stronger protection for victims; support carers with a national Respite Care Programme and higher social service standards; regenerate the worst estates; universal nursery vouchers for all four-year-olds and an Education Guarantee; Citizen’s Charter to ensure fair service standards; welfare into work with obligations; low taxes and rewards for work; defend the UK and resist EU federalism with a referendum before any single currency entry. TEXT_G: secure a national debate and hold a referendum on the EU relationship; offer a choice between a federal Europe and an association of sovereign nations; hold the vote before or alongside the next General Election; reject limiting any referendum to the single currency and insist on addressing sovereignty.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate profile weights Care and Equality most, with some Proportionality; TEXT_F offers multiple concrete care commitments (NHS, carers, victims, estates, environment) and some universal/equal‑treatment measures, plus proportionality through rewarding work, while also touching Loyalty at a moderate level. TEXT_G focuses largely on sovereignty and procedural democracy (Loyalty/Authority), which are lower‑priority foundations in the profile, and offers little on Care or Equality, so TEXT_F aligns better overall.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
